# Database migrations (strict)

This project uses **Supabase Postgres** accessed through the **Supabase JS client**
(`@supabase/supabase-js`). There is no ORM. The server-only admin client (service-role key, bypasses
RLS) lives in `src/lib/supabase.ts`; authorization is handled in app code via `src/lib/permissions.ts`,
not Supabase RLS. Hand-maintained row/insert/update types live in `src/lib/database.types.ts`.

## Required

- **Every schema change ships as a forward-only SQL migration** under `supabase/migrations/`, named
  `<UTC timestamp>_<description>.sql` (e.g. `20260812120300_add_document_archived.sql`). The 14-digit
  timestamp prefix is not cosmetic — `supabase db push` ignores files that don't match it, so a
  migration named `0004_foo.sql` silently never runs. `supabase migration new <name>` generates the
  prefix for you.
- Apply migrations via the Supabase Dashboard → SQL Editor, or the Supabase CLI (`supabase db push`).
- **Never edit, rename, or delete a migration that has already been applied.** Applied migrations are
  immutable history. To change something, add a **new** migration that moves the state forward.
- Keep `src/lib/database.types.ts` in sync with the schema (or regenerate it with
  `supabase gen types typescript` if you adopt the Supabase CLI).
- Enable Row-Level Security on new tables. Server access uses the service-role key (bypasses RLS), so
  a table with RLS on and no policies is safe by default.

## Avoid

- ❌ Hand-editing SQL in an already-applied `supabase/migrations/<file>.sql`.
- ❌ Deleting a migration to "redo" it after it has been applied to any shared database.
- ❌ Importing `src/lib/supabase.ts` (service-role admin client) into client components — it must stay
  server-side.

## Prefer

```sql
-- Made a schema change? Add the next migration:
-- supabase/migrations/20260812120300_add_document_archived.sql
alter table public.documents add column archived_at timestamptz;

-- Need to undo/adjust an applied change? New migration, forward:
-- supabase/migrations/20260812120400_drop_document_archived.sql
alter table public.documents drop column archived_at;
```

When in doubt: add a new migration, never touch an old one.
